#
# THE BAND'S FILE AND TIME HOST, ON THE BOARD.
#
# A CADR has no file or time server in it.  The fabric has none, and
# cadr-chaosnet has none: it is the cable and nothing above it.  The host a
# band calls is a machine on the network, and a board with no network reached
# no such machine, so it booted, painted its window system and said its file
# host was not a known host --- and it did not know the date either.  This
# script starts one on the board itself, so that a board with a card and
# nothing plugged into it is a whole site.
#
# **IT IS ON UNLESS THE CARD SAYS OTHERWISE.**  `--no-ozd` turns it off, in the
# spelling this board already uses for a thing that is on until somebody says
# no: `--no-auto-boot` and `--no-blinking-leds`.  Every other setting is
# spelled `--ozd-`, because a word like `--root`, `--host`, `--name` or
# `--port` is a word another program could want, and the card's one file of
# flags cannot carry a word two programs answer to.
#
# S84: after S80cadr-disk-packs, which is the one thing that mounts the
# partition this file of flags is on, and before S87cadr-chaosnet, so that the
# machine's first request for the time finds a host already listening.  Not
# last, so S88cadr-usb-input keeps the report of the lines no program claimed.
#
# **IT LISTENS ON THE LOOPBACK AND NOWHERE ELSE, AND THAT IS A DECISION.**  ozd
# authenticates nobody: any host that can reach its socket may read every root
# it serves and write every root that is not marked read-only.  On the loopback
# the only thing that can reach it is this board, which is the whole of what
# was asked for.  It also settles the address.  Every board runs a copy at the
# same Chaosnet address, because the address is not free to choose --- it is
# whatever the band's own host table calls its file host --- and three boards
# on one network are still not three hosts claiming one address, because none
# of them claims it on the network.  The address is a name inside one board.
#
# **AND A BOARD THAT ALREADY HAS A FILE HOST TURNS THIS ONE OFF.**  A band
# calls ONE address for its file host.  If the card names a peer at that
# address on a real network and this host answers at the same address on the
# loopback, then cadr-chaosnet is given one Chaos address at two endpoints and
# refuses it by name --- `udp: 177200 twice; one endpoint an address` --- and
# the board is left with no cable at all.  So S87cadr-chaosnet reads what this
# script leaves behind and a card that places that address itself keeps its own
# host and is told so on the console.  `--no-ozd` is the other way to say it,
# and it is the one to use when the host on the network is the one that should
# win.

# Where the card is mounted, which `S80cadr-disk-packs` is the one thing
# that does.  The card is one FAT32 partition and `fpgarc` is at its root.
CARD=/mnt/card
PROG=/usr/bin/ozd
PIDFILE=/var/run/ozd.pid
LOG=/var/log/ozd.log

# **WHAT S87cadr-chaosnet READS**: one line, the peer entry that reaches this
# host, in that program's own syntax.  It is written only when the host is
# really running and removed whenever it is not, so it is a fact about this
# boot rather than a second copy of a setting.  /var/run is a RAM disk unpacked
# at every boot, so it starts absent and cannot go stale across a reset; it is
# removed at the top of start() all the same, because a `restart` can leave one
# standing and a marker that outlives its host is a lie about the board.
PEERFILE=/var/run/cadr-ozd.peer

RC="$CARD/fpgarc"

# The reader, cadr-common's, installed by that package on the target.  Its
# header is the contract.  If it is not there this script dies here and the
# shell names the file, which is the loud failure and the right one.
FPGARC_SH=/usr/share/cadr/fpgarc.sh
. "$FPGARC_SH"

# And the stopper, cadr-common's too: `start-stop-daemon -K` sends SIGTERM and
# returns at once, so `stop` waits for the program to be gone before it says OK
# and before anything after it runs.  Its header is the whole account.
STOP_SH=/usr/share/cadr/stop.sh
. "$STOP_SH"

# **THE FLAGS THIS SCRIPT OWNS**, each one of ozd's own under a name this board
# can carry.  The mapping is one for one and `to_ozd` below is the whole of it:
#
#     --no-ozd            this board's, and not ozd's: do not start it at all
#     --ozd-chaos-address ozd's --address
#     --ozd-name          ozd's --name
#     --ozd-port          the port of ozd's --listen, on the loopback
#     --ozd-root          ozd's --root, repeatable
#     --ozd-host          ozd's --host, repeatable
#     --ozd-hosts-text    ozd's --hosts-text
#     --ozd-trace         ozd's --trace
#
# **AND ozd'S `--listen` IS NOT OFFERED WHOLE, ONLY ITS PORT.**  An endpoint
# flag would let a card put this host on a network, where it would serve every
# root to anybody who could reach the port, with no authentication of any kind
# and no way to tell afterwards.  That is not a setting somebody should be able
# to make by uncommenting a line with the card in a reader.  A board that is to
# serve a second machine is a decision taken with the program's own flags, and
# this script is not the place for it.
#
# **AND NONE OF ozd'S FIVE `--log-` FLAGS IS HERE.**  The log is a file in the
# root filesystem, which is a RAM disk, and a log that grew without bound would
# take the board down.  ozd is quiet unless asked --- a whole boot leaves a
# line or two --- and asking is what those flags do.
FLAGS="--no-ozd
       --ozd-chaos-address
       --ozd-name
       --ozd-port
       --ozd-root
       --ozd-host
       --ozd-hosts-text
       --ozd-trace"

# **THE DEFAULTS, AND WHY EACH IS WHAT IT IS.**
#
# THE ADDRESS IS THE ONE THE BAND CALLS, and it is not free to choose.  A band
# holds a host table and calls its file and time host at the address that table
# gives; a host answering anywhere else is a host the band never calls, and a
# service that runs and is never reached looks exactly like one that works.  So
# the default is the address the band this project ships beside names for its
# own associated machine, and a board running another band says so on the card:
# System 100's table calls 3060 and System 304's calls 4403.
DEFAULT_ADDRESS=177200
# ITS NAMES, as a band's host table writes them: the official name first, then
# the system type.  STATUS answers with the first of them.
DEFAULT_NAME=OZ,system=UNIX
# THE PORT.  CHUDP's own port is 42042 and the CADR in the fabric takes it, so
# this host cannot have it --- two programs on one board cannot bind one port
# --- and the CADR inside muir already took 42043 in muirrc.  42142 is this
# project's own number for this service and has been since the copy that runs
# beside the boards took it, which is one number for one thing.  On the
# loopback it can never meet that copy.
DEFAULT_PORT=42142
# THE BASE ROOT, which is where a user's home directory lives.  ozd requires at
# least one root and refuses to start without one.  LOGIN gives each user the
# home directory /<user>/ in lower case, and a band that compiles a system
# writes its compiler warnings there and stops with "Access to file denied" if
# it cannot, so this has to exist and be writable.  It is in the root
# filesystem, which is a RAM disk unpacked at every boot, so it costs the card
# nothing and it is empty at every boot.
#
# **IT IS ALWAYS PASSED, AND A CARD'S OWN ROOTS ARE MOUNTS BESIDE IT.**  That
# is the one place this parts from the rule that a script's default stands only
# where the card is silent, and the reason is that a home directory is not a
# setting: it is a thing that has to exist.  A card names a tree with
# `--ozd-root <name>=<path>,ro`, which ozd mounts at /<name>.  A card that
# writes an unnamed root instead gets ozd's own refusal by name, because a
# second base is not a thing ozd has.
BASE_ROOT=/var/lib/ozd/lispm
# THE USER.  ozd refuses to run as root, in its own words, and it is right to:
# nothing it does needs a privilege, and as root a path check it got wrong
# would reach the whole filesystem.  Buildroot's users table makes this user
# and the directory above belongs to it.
OZD_USER=ozd

# **WHERE THE TREE IT SERVES LIVES, WHICH IS NOWHERE BY DEFAULT.**  The band's
# sources are about 16 MiB.  The root filesystem is a RAM disk unpacked at
# every boot, so a tree put there costs that much memory on every board whether
# this host is ever asked for a file or not --- and this host is on by default.
# So the default serves no tree at all, and what that costs is nothing.
#
# What a board gets for nothing is the two things that were actually stopping
# it: the time, which the machine asks for at every boot and otherwise has to
# be told by hand, and the host table, which is what makes the band's file host
# a known host rather than an error raised before a single packet is sent.
#
# A tree is named on the card, and the place for it is the card itself,
# which is on the card and costs no memory:
#
#     --ozd-root sys=/mnt/card/sys,ro
#
# docs/chaosnet.md has the measured sizes behind that and behind the two other
# places it could have gone.

# The argument of one of this script's own flags, or nothing.  Asking claims
# the flag, which is right: a script only asks about a flag it owns.
value_of() {
	eval "set -- $(fpgarc_args "$RC" "$1")"
	[ $# -ge 2 ] && printf '%s\n' "$2"
}

# The card's lines in this board's spelling, printed as ozd's own words,
# shell-quoted for `eval`.  This is the whole of the mapping and there is no
# other copy of it.  `--no-ozd` and `--ozd-port` are dropped here because they
# are answered before this runs: the first stops the host and the second is
# folded into `--listen`.
to_ozd() {
	while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
		case "$1" in
		--ozd-chaos-address)
			fpgarc_quote --address; fpgarc_quote "$2"; shift 2 ;;
		--ozd-name)
			fpgarc_quote --name; fpgarc_quote "$2"; shift 2 ;;
		--ozd-root)
			fpgarc_quote --root; fpgarc_quote "$2"; shift 2 ;;
		--ozd-host)
			fpgarc_quote --host; fpgarc_quote "$2"; shift 2 ;;
		--ozd-hosts-text)
			fpgarc_quote --hosts-text; fpgarc_quote "$2"; shift 2 ;;
		--ozd-trace)
			fpgarc_quote --trace; shift ;;
		*)
			shift ;;
		esac
	done
}

start() {
	rm -f "$PEERFILE"

	if fpgarc_has "$RC" --no-ozd; then
		echo "ozd: --no-ozd on the card: this board serves itself no files and no time."
		echo "ozd: the band's file host is whichever --chaos-udp-peer names, on the network."
		# Every other flag of this script's is claimed all the same, so
		# that an --ozd- setting left beside --no-ozd is not reported at
		# S88 as a line no program takes.  It did reach a program: this
		# one, which was told not to run.
		fpgarc_args "$RC" $FLAGS > /dev/null
		return 0
	fi

	address=$(value_of --ozd-chaos-address)
	[ -n "$address" ] || address=$DEFAULT_ADDRESS
	port=$(value_of --ozd-port)
	[ -n "$port" ] || port=$DEFAULT_PORT

	# The home directory, made before ozd looks at it and owned by the user
	# that will write in it.  ozd refuses a root it cannot resolve, so it
	# has to exist rather than be made on first use.
	mkdir -p "$BASE_ROOT" 2>/dev/null
	chown -R "$OZD_USER" "$BASE_ROOT" 2>/dev/null

	# The defaults first and the card's lines after them, so that a card
	# line wins, which is muir's rule and the rule everywhere in this file.
	set -- --address "$DEFAULT_ADDRESS" --name "$DEFAULT_NAME" \
	       --listen "127.0.0.1:$port" --root "$BASE_ROOT"
	card=$(fpgarc_args "$RC" $FLAGS)
	eval "set -- \"\$@\" $(eval "to_ozd $card")"

	# **THE FLAGS ARE CHECKED BEFORE THE DAEMON IS STARTED, AND ozd HAS ITS
	# OWN WORD FOR IT.**  `--check` reads the flags, checks the roots and
	# exits without changing anything.  That is better here than this
	# board's usual trick of starting a daemon and running it again when it
	# is not there: it is the program's own dry run, it happens before a
	# socket is bound, and it costs nothing when it passes.  What it prints
	# when it fails is the refusal, in ozd's own words, on the console.
	#
	# `-c /dev/null` so that the run depends on this card and on nothing
	# else: without it ozd reads .ozdrc from the working directory or from
	# a home directory, and an init script's working directory is not a
	# thing anybody meant to configure a board with.
	if ! "$PROG" --check -c /dev/null "$@" > "$PIDFILE.check" 2>&1; then
		echo "Starting ozd: FAIL"
		sed 's/^/ozd: /' "$PIDFILE.check"
		rm -f "$PIDFILE.check"
		return 1
	fi
	rm -f "$PIDFILE.check"

	printf "Starting ozd: "
	# `-c` runs it as its own user.  The `sh -c exec` is what keeps its
	# log: `-b` daemonizes and sends stdout and stderr to /dev/null, and
	# ozd logs to stderr and takes no flag naming a destination.  `exec`
	# replaces the shell with ozd, so the pid start-stop-daemon wrote is
	# ozd's own and `stop` below kills the right process rather than a
	# shell that is no longer there.
	if start-stop-daemon -S -q -b -m -p "$PIDFILE" -c "$OZD_USER" \
	    --exec /bin/sh -- -c "exec \"\$0\" -c /dev/null \"\$@\" >> $LOG 2>&1" \
	    "$PROG" "$@"; then
		sleep 1
		p=$(cat "$PIDFILE" 2>/dev/null)
		if [ -n "$p" ] && kill -0 "$p" 2>/dev/null; then
			echo "OK"
			echo "$address@127.0.0.1:$port" > "$PEERFILE"
			echo "ozd: this board is its own file and time host, at $address" \
			     "on the loopback at port $port."
			return 0
		fi
	fi
	echo "FAIL"
	tail -5 "$LOG" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/ozd: /'
	return 1
}

stop() {
	cadr_stop ozd "$PIDFILE"
	rm -f "$PEERFILE"
}

case "$1" in
	start) start ;;
	stop) stop ;;
	restart|reload) stop; start ;;
	*) echo "Usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}"; exit 1 ;;
esac

exit $?
